<h2><%= l(:label_reporter_template_new) %></h2>

<%#
  T-37 / FR-73 — THE GALLERY COMES FIRST, and only on this page.

  §9b.1: *"Start from an example, never from empty."* So the five starters are above the
  form when nothing has been started from yet, and once one has been applied the gallery
  collapses to one line naming it — because at that point the author's attention belongs to
  the code, and five links that would replace what they are looking at do not.

  `@starter` is set by the controller only for a starter it recognises; an unknown
  `?starter=` id leaves it nil and puts a warning in the flash rather than showing a blank
  form to somebody who asked for an example.
%>
<% if @starter %>
  <p class="icon icon-ok" id="reporter-starter-applied">
    <%= l(:text_reporter_starter_applied, name: l(@starter.name_key)) %>
    <%= link_to l(:label_reporter_starter_choose_another),
                new_project_reporter_template_path(@project) %>
  </p>
<% else %>
  <%= render partial: 'starter_gallery' %>
<% end %>

<%#
  PREVIEW BEFORE THE FIRST SAVE, which is when it is worth most: §9b.2's whole argument is
  that an author should find out at the keyboard that their template breaks in the PDF.
  Previewing only after saving would mean the first thing a new template does is get
  stored. The button is in `_editor` with the rest; nothing is written by pressing it.
%>
<%= render partial: 'editor',
           locals: { url: project_reporter_templates_path(@project),
                     method: :post,
                     preview_url: preview_project_reporter_templates_path(@project),
                     submit_label: l(:button_create) } %>
